<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
   Cleartext policy.

   The app talks to two kinds of host:

     1. YouTube / Gaana CDNs — always HTTPS. Nothing here should ever need to weaken this.
     2. An optional self-hosted GaanaPy server (`settings.source.gaana_base_url`), which a
        developer may run locally over plain http.

   The default is therefore `cleartextTrafficPermitted="false"` — Android 9+ already blocks
   cleartext, and this makes that explicit rather than implicit. Cleartext is re-enabled only
   for the loopback and emulator aliases below, which cannot leave the device or the host
   machine and so cannot be intercepted on a network.

   ## Adding a LAN server

   If your GaanaPy instance runs on another machine over http (e.g. `http://192.168.1.42:8000`),
   its literal address must be listed here — Android's config is static and does not accept
   wildcards or runtime values, so the base-URL setting alone cannot grant it access. Add a
   `domain-config` block of the same shape as the one below with the real IP:

       <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
           <domain includeSubdomains="false">192.168.1.42</domain>
       </domain-config>

   Prefer a tunnel (ngrok, Cloudflare Tunnel, Tailscale) over a LAN IP where possible: that
   gives you HTTPS and needs no change here at all. Never widen this to a base-config-level
   cleartext allowance — that would permit every host in the app to be downgraded.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />

    <!-- Loopback and the Android emulator's alias for the host machine. -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
